\p{Beginning the Triton experience...}
\p{Immediately, I understand the notion of tiling but do not actually remember how to implement it.}
\question{Does the grid always divide up the output tensor?}
\answer{No, see the next question.}
\question{How do we keep the accumulated results needed from [online softmax](PI7N)?}
\answer{For loop inside the kernel. We do not divide up the output tensor but loop over it in block size chunks.}

\p{\strong{8:40 AM}}
\hr
\p{Finished writing the online softmax kernel in [triton](tilletTritonIntermediateLanguage2019). In order to do the accumulation, you do need to do a for loop within the kernel. However, you can perform blockwise \strong{associative} operations. An example of this is the max and sum in the [online softmax](PI7N) can be done at the block level.}
\p{Overall, this is definitely much slower than when we are able to do the softmax across the entire row at once, so it is an example of just how expensive it is to round trip through main memory. }

